cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "NeuroGenesis: provable identifiability and the cost of symbol grounding"
message: "If you use this software or its findings, please cite it."
type: software
authors:
  - alias: jaisurya93945
repository-code: "https://github.com/jaisurya93945/NeuroGenesis"
abstract: >-
  A preregistered study of reasoning shortcuts in neuro-symbolic learning. The
  size of the reasoning-shortcut set, computed offline before training, predicts
  whether a model grounds its concepts, and the oracle predicts which wrong
  grounding a converged model adopts. Selecting auxiliary tasks by shortcut
  coverage collapses that set optimally and still loses on cost to plain concept
  supervision -- a negative answer to an open question posed by the 2026 JAIR
  survey on symbol grounding. Includes a triple-validated shortcut oracle, all
  raw run records, and the preregistrations committed before each experiment ran.
keywords:
  - neuro-symbolic
  - reasoning shortcuts
  - symbol grounding
  - identifiability
  - preregistration
  - negative results
license: MIT
